  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 248 AM CDT Wed Oct 21 2026\n\nTODAY...There is quite the range in regional temperatures this \nmorning, with parts of western Minnesota still sitting in the \nlow 50s while some spots in west-central Wisconsin are \nplummeting to the low 30s already. There is also seemingly \nrandom 10 degree differences over short distances all across the\narea. For example, KLVN in Lakeville is sitting at 45F at 2AM \nand KMSP is at a balmy 55F. Maybe that example is influenced by \nthe urban heat island, but you can find more examples of that\nspread all over this morning. This is likely showing the battle\nbetween southerly winds advecting warm air northwards and clear\nskies facilitating efficient radiational cooling (strength \nvarying based on location). All that to say, some folks will be \nwaking up to chilly temps this morning. Winds will not increase \ntoo drastically today, though the persistent southerly flow and \nsunny skies will bump highs up a couple degrees from yesterday. \n\nTHURSDAY TO SATURDAY...Temperatures will run a good 15 to 20\ndegrees above normal for mid-October (meaning highs in the mid \n70s and lows in the mid 50s). Both Thursday and Friday have a \nshot at exceeding 80 degrees for locations along the Buffalo \nRidge. Southeasterly winds will increase for Friday and Saturday\nahead of a developing low pressure system over southern Canada.\nA cold front is expected to swing through the region on \nSaturday, though not before it loses much of its umph. The \nsoutherly flow will help increase PWATs to a little over an inch\n(which is almost 3 times normal for mid October), so there will\ncertainly be enough moisture to tap into. The overall lack of \nstrong forcing though will likely limit precip amounts to a \ncouple tenths at most as the rounds of showers move through this\nweekend.\n\nSUNDAY TO TUESDAY...The pattern changes and our late summer-like \nweather comes to an end next week. The highs next week will be \nsimilar to what our lows are like this week. And lows drop into \nthe 30s, with many areas across the north likely seeing sub-\nfreezing temps again as we near the end of October. Rain chances\nlinger to start the week, though it depends on how far south \nthat front is able to drop.",
